{"id": "e0867f3f-7d8e-49fb-ae67-28a7574bfd30", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This is a broad production-readiness survey across the account, so it's better delegated to background subagents rather than done token-inefficiently myself. I'm thinking it could split into roughly two agents—one covering volumes, snapshots, encryption, and backups via the EC2 API, and another covering CloudWatch metrics like burst balance and IOPS plus AWS Backup/DLM coverage.

I'll keep it simple with two parallel subagents: one for configuration (volumes, encryption, types, snapshots, DLM, AWS Backup) and one for performance metrics (CloudWatch). I'm not sure which accounts are in scope beyond the agent space account, but the subagents can check associations themselves, so I'll just dispatch both, preloading the metrics-exploration skill for the second one, and let the user know my plan.

"}]}

{"id": "e5f93b7a-5f5b-421b-bcd0-d791794efef8", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review your EBS setup in us-east-1 from two angles at once: configuration and data protection, then performance and capacity. The configuration review covers volume types, encryption, snapshot and backup coverage, and orphaned or unattached volumes. The performance review covers burst balance, IOPS and throughput headroom, and queue depth. I'll bring the results together into a readiness verdict.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_cwjSpxBoHztMbD3Z0p7owU", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-config-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Production-readiness review of EBS configuration and data protection in us-east-1. Read-only: do not make any changes.",
  "context": "User has a launch next week and wants to know whether their EBS storage is production-ready. Cover every AWS account associated with the agent space (use list_associations to find them; agent space account is 012345678901_1) in region us-east-1. This is a first-pass (shallow) survey: return concise findings and suggest deeper areas.",
  "resources": ["All EBS volumes, snapshots, AWS Backup plans, and DLM lifecycle policies in us-east-1"],
  "timeframe": "Current state (now is 2026-11-23T19:44Z)",
  "criteria": "Report: (1) volume inventory counts by type/size/state and attachment (which instances, tag Name/Environment where available); (2) encryption status of volumes and whether EBS encryption-by-default is enabled in the region, and the KMS key used (AWS-managed vs customer-managed); (3) snapshot/backup coverage: which volumes have recent snapshots (within 24h/7d), whether DLM policies or AWS Backup plans cover them, and any volumes with no backup; (4) legacy or suboptimal volume types (gp2, standard, io1 where gp3/io2 would be better), and gp3 volumes at baseline IOPS/throughput that may need tuning; (5) unattached/available volumes, volumes with DeleteOnTermination=true on data volumes, Multi-Attach usage; (6) old/orphaned snapshots, public or shared snapshots, snapshot archive/Recycle Bin rules; (7) any fast snapshot restore config. Provide a short prioritized list of risks (High/Medium/Low) with specific resource IDs, plus counts. Keep output compact."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["verifying-with-docs"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS configuration and backup coverage in us-east-1"}, {"id": "tooluse_MOMs4GurPxhGNesp7MYZQY", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-perf-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Assess EBS performance headroom and health for volumes in us-east-1 using CloudWatch metrics. Read-only: do not make any changes.",
  "context": "User has a launch next week and wants to know whether their EBS storage is production-ready. Cover every AWS account associated with the agent space (use list_associations; agent space account is 012345678901_1), region us-east-1. First-pass (shallow): focus on attached in-use volumes, prioritizing the busiest ones, and return concise findings.",
  "resources": ["EBS volumes in us-east-1 (AWS/EBS namespace), plus EC2 instance-level EBS limits for attached instances"],
  "timeframe": "Last 14 days ending 2026-11-23T19:44:00Z",
  "criteria": "Report: (1) volumes with burst balance (gp2/st1/sc1) low or depleting; (2) volumes where peak IOPS or throughput approaches provisioned/baseline limits (gp3 baseline 3000 IOPS/125 MiB/s unless provisioned higher; io1/io2 provisioned IOPS) — show peak vs limit as a percentage; (3) VolumeQueueLength sustained high, elevated read/write latency (derived from VolumeTotalReadTime/WriteTime over ops); (4) VolumeStatus / EBS status check impaired or stalled-IO indications (VolumeStalledIOCheck), and any CloudWatch alarms in ALARM state related to EBS; (5) instance-level EBS bandwidth/IOPS limit proximity (EBSReadOps/EBSWriteOps/EBSIOBalance%/EBSByteBalance% on Nitro instances); (6) whether CloudWatch alarms exist for EBS (burst balance, queue length, stalled IO) — note gaps; (7) any file-system capacity concerns if CloudWatch agent disk metrics (disk_used_percent) exist. Return a compact prioritized list of risks (High/Medium/Low) with specific volume/instance IDs and numbers."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS performance headroom via CloudWatch in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "6dbe5d67-72b7-4925-88a5-2e1761c6d2c6", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "With 22 in-use volumes, I'm planning to pull ReadOps and WriteOps metrics via get_metric_data across a 14-day window, checking the datapoint limits to make sure I batch the queries efficiently per volume.

Given the 100k datapoint cap, batching 3-4 volumes per request with 300s period seems safest, even though it means around 8 separate requests. Querying with a coarser period would smooth out the peaks I actually care about, so I'll stick with 300s and rely on jmespath's max() to compress results server-side before they come back to me. Worth checking first whether many of these volumes are essentially idle, which would simplify things.

"}], "parent_id": "9d9d3923-4ad7-412a-b868-71250cb47e03"}

{"id": "89cf0ca0-e2f4-4fb2-b60f-2b13ecd1878c", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Looking at the volumes sorted by timestamp, I see vol-0690 hitting 125 MiB/s throughput against its 125 baseline—fully saturated—while IOPS sits at 500 of 3000. Vol-0493 is running at 86% of its IOPS limit with moderate throughput and a queue depth of 16. I want to pin down when these peaks occurred and how long they lasted, then check the rest of the volumes by computing max IOPS and throughput for each in-use one more efficiently.

I'm trying to figure out the best way to extract just the timestamps and durations of saturation without pulling the entire 186-point series twice over—since the data looks sparse, it's possible these volumes are mostly idle and only report datapoints when active. I'll just pull the full queue, IOPS, and MiB/s values for these two volumes directly to see the pattern.

"}], "parent_id": "9d9d3923-4ad7-412a-b868-71250cb47e03"}

{"id": "f0cf2f7c-0509-4649-982d-bed99f374b59", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm double-checking that volume's IOPS figure against its 16000 baseline—roughly 74% utilized—and want to cross-reference throughput and latency for the top volumes, noting that the gp2 volume at 12GB only supports 100 baseline IOPS.

Several gp2 volumes show no metrics at all, which could mean their instances are stopped, so I should check instance states, instance types for relevant limits, status checks, and any VolumeStalledIOCheck flags.

"}], "parent_id": "9d9d3923-4ad7-412a-b868-71250cb47e03"}

{"id": "d73a1b69-f231-4db3-aca2-b0fd8bdcae0d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Gp3 alarm d just flipped to OK, and alarm b's read bytes work out to roughly 125 MiB/s, which is fine. More notably, vol-0690 is attached to i-04ec, a c6i.xlarge that's EBS-optimized by default even though the flag shows false.

c6i.xlarge has a 1.25 Gbps baseline bandwidth with burst up to 12,500 Mbps, so that's not a bottleneck. But t3.micro i-0cf83 with vol-00612 provisioned at 16,000 IOPS gp3 looks suspicious -- t3.micro's max burst is only around 11,800 IOPS / 2,085 Mbps, and the observed peak of 11,805 IOPS matches that ceiling almost exactly. That strongly suggests the instance itself is the bottleneck, not the volume's provisioned IOPS. I want to confirm this by pulling EBSIOBalance%, EBSByteBalance%, EBSReadOps/WriteOps across the running instances (t3.micro, t3.medium, c6i.xlarge, c6i.large, m5.xlarge) along with throughput, latency, and queue depth on vol-00612 around the time of that peak.

"}], "parent_id": "9d9d3923-4ad7-412a-b868-71250cb47e03"}

{"id": "b52fc49a-f466-4dec-becf-333520cca511", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice a volume status event flagged as severely-degraded I/O on vol-1234567890abcdef0_11, though the description oddly reads as normal throughput — worth flagging that discrepancy while reporting it. I'm also noting the EBSIOBalance% metric for instance i-0cf83, a t3.micro, as a key finding to dig into.

Instance burst credits hit zero on i-0cf83, meaning it exhausted its IO burst balance, and I want to cross-check bandwidth/IOPS baselines against AWS documentation for the c6i.xlarge, c6i.large, and t3.micro instance types to confirm whether observed peaks exceed their baseline limits. I should also pull the timing and duration of the zero-balance event on i-0cf83, along with throughput, queue depth, and latency metrics for the related volume.

"}], "parent_id": "9d9d3923-4ad7-412a-b868-71250cb47e03"}

{"id": "1fea7ca2-c717-4e8f-89d1-28df8487d194", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Instance i-0cf83's IO balance has stayed pegged at 0% for over 100 minutes through 19:39, meaning its burst credits are exhausted and it's throttled to baseline 500 IOPS even though the volume is provisioned for 16000 IOPS — a mismatch worth flagging. Now I want to check latency, looking at ReadTime/WriteTime per operation across the top volumes.

Let me quickly calculate average latency for 0690, 049399, 00612, and 00dbff (io2) by taking (read time + write time) divided by (read ops + write ops) in seconds, converting to milliseconds, and checking the max across periods.

"}], "parent_id": "9d9d3923-4ad7-412a-b868-71250cb47e03"}

{"id": "d106ae0c-8b0a-4cb5-afa6-96229e43bf5d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice vol-049399 has a pretty alarming max latency of 380ms per operation. The volume status check confirms io-enabled passed though, so I'll note that stalled IO wasn't queried metrically but status looks fine, and move on to checking unattached/available volumes.

There are also 7 unattached volumes worth flagging as a cost concern, and 27 of 28 volumes are unencrypted. Looking closer at vol-049399 on i-05c63 (c6i.large), it's hitting around 86% of the gp3 3000 IOPS limit with a queue depth of 16, which likely explains the latency spikes even though instance-level IOPS headroom seems fine. For vol-0690, throughput is steady at 125 MiB/s (100% of baseline) with low queue depth and max latency of just 6.6ms, well within the instance's burst capacity — no issues there.

"}], "parent_id": "9d9d3923-4ad7-412a-b868-71250cb47e03"}

